1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,678
Chapter six. Gold: is there money without a cave?

2
00:00:05,828 --> 00:00:20,815
Every money in this episode had a Wren. A ship on Yap. Glass from Venice. Iron drills. Shells from Zanzibar. A mint in Rome. Each time, money got easier to make. Usually, the savers paid.

3
00:00:21,765 --> 00:00:32,823
Now gold. All the gold ever mined would fit in one cube, about 23 metres on each side. As tall as a seven-storey building. Almost all of it still exists.

4
00:00:33,173 --> 00:00:38,990
Each year, the world's mines add a slice on top, less than half a metre thick. Under 2%.

5
00:00:41,539 --> 00:00:55,353
Gold had its rushes. California in 1849, then Australia. In the 1850s, the world dug over three times as much gold a year as in the 1840s. Three times a thin slice is still a thin slice.

6
00:00:55,703 --> 00:01:08,825
Prices rose. In Britain, where money was gold, by up to a third. They stayed high for about twenty years. Then they fell. By the 1890s, they were lower than in the 1840s.

7
00:01:09,775 --> 00:01:27,396
Compare. Cowries: a silver dollar cost 10 times more shells. Rome: wheat cost 37 times more. Gold, in the great rushes: prices up to a third higher, for a while. Gold came closest to money without a cave.

8
00:01:28,746 --> 00:01:43,967
And today? In ten years, gold's price roughly tripled, with big swings. So digging it pays far more. Still, mine output grew under 1% a year. Nobody has found gold's cave.

9
00:01:45,716 --> 00:01:56,209
Our money today isn't gold. So who is its Wren? First: why is gold so hard to flood? Next episode: why gold.

